Clear the epoll read-ready state when a TLS handshake would block

In epoll mode a connection's read-ready bit is set by an EPOLLIN event and
cleared only by the TLS record-read adapter when gnutls_record_recv()
returns GNUTLS_E_AGAIN.  While a connection is still handshaking that
adapter is never reached: MHD_connection_handle_read() calls
gnutls_handshake() first, which does its own recv(), gets EAGAIN, and
MHD_run_tls_handshake_() returns with the bit still set.  The connection
therefore stays on the epoll ready list, which makes the daemon poll with a
zero timeout, and every pass calls gnutls_handshake() again.  On a TLS 1.3
server that recomputes the expected Finished (transcript hash, HKDF, HMAC)
on each call.  The spin lasts one client round trip for every handshake,
and for a client that never completes the handshake it lasts until the
connection timeout.

GNUTLS_E_AGAIN from gnutls_handshake() means its pull or push function got
EAGAIN, so the socket is drained (or its send buffer is full) until epoll
reports it again; clear the bit for the direction it is waiting on, as the
record adapter already does for reads.

--- a/src/microhttpd/connection_https.c
+++ b/src/microhttpd/connection_https.c
@@ -156,6 +156,21 @@ MHD_run_tls_handshake_ (struct MHD_Connection *connection)
     if ( (GNUTLS_E_AGAIN == ret) ||
          (GNUTLS_E_INTERRUPTED == ret) )
     {
+#ifdef EPOLL_SUPPORT
+      /* gnutls got EAGAIN from the socket in the direction it reports, so
+       * nothing more can happen there until epoll signals it again.  Leaving
+       * the ready bit set keeps the connection on the eready list, which
+       * makes the daemon busy-wait, re-running the handshake on every pass. */
+      if (GNUTLS_E_AGAIN == ret)
+      {
+        if (0 == gnutls_record_get_direction (connection->tls_session))
+          connection->epoll_state &=
+            ~((enum MHD_EpollState) MHD_EPOLL_STATE_READ_READY);
+        else
+          connection->epoll_state &=
+            ~((enum MHD_EpollState) MHD_EPOLL_STATE_WRITE_READY);
+      }
+#endif /* EPOLL_SUPPORT */
       connection->tls_state = MHD_TLS_CONN_HANDSHAKING;
       /* handshake not done */
       return false;
